</div>

<div class="page">
    <h3 style="margin-top: 40px;">Recent performance measurements</h3>
    <p>In mid-2012, ABB conducted a series of measurements to ensure that the continuing improvements in DTC technology are keeping ABB AC drives at the top of performance. Significant results of the test measurements are summarized here.</p>
    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 30px;">
        <div style="width: 48%;">
            <img src="placeholder_graph_1.png" alt="Torque Stability" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto;">
            <p class="figure-caption">Graph 1: Both drives have remarkable sensorless control ability to operate large pend loads in the near zero speed range. The new ACS880 has less deviation from the torque reference and thus can provide better motor control performance than ACS800.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="width: 48%;">
            <img src="placeholder_graph_2.png" alt="Torque Deviation" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto;">
            <p class="figure-caption">Graph 2: For both motor types the torque deviation from reference is typically 1-5% at low speeds, and the torque response time is typically 1-5 milliseconds (ms). This is very close to the theoretical maximum. The torque response time for the induction motor is typically 1-5 ms, while the SynRM motor is typically 1-3 ms.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top: 30px;">Torque stability near zero speed</p>
    <p>Graph 1 compares the torque control accuracy of ACS880 and new ACS880 industrial drives in sensorless (open-loop) operating mode. In the tests, the new ACS880 has less deviation from the torque reference and thus can provide better motor control performance than ACS800.</p>
    <p>Servo-class dynamic performance</p>
    <p>Graph 3 indicates measured speed and angular position of a motor driven by the new ACS880 drive. The torque response time is typically 1-5 milliseconds (ms). This is very close to the theoretical maximum. The torque response time for the induction motor is typically 1-5 ms, while the SynRM motor is typically 1-3 ms.</p>
    <p>Torque accuracy during ramping</p>
    <p>Graph 2 shows the ACS880 drive’s sensorless torque control accuracy for two types of 15 kW motors tested (at 50% of rated speed) – a four-pole induction motor and a synchronous reluctance motor.</p>
    <p>Graph 3. Although not a servo drive, ACS880 drive with DTC can change motor speed very fast and accurately both with closed loop and sensorless control. The torque response time is typically 1-5 ms, which is very close to the theoretical maximum. The torque response time for the induction motor is typically 1-5 ms, while the SynRM motor is typically 1-3 ms, respectively, which correspond to acceleration times for absolute torque accuracy.</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
    <h3 style="margin-top: 40px;">Wider applications</h3>
    <p>Another aspect of the DTC story is its expansion beyond applications for which the technology was created.</p>
    <p>Demanding, high-dynamic applications like those in machine tools, marine propulsion, wind turbines (generators), and pumps, are all benefiting from the DTC technology. The technology has changed greatly. Control system software has been significantly improved, and the DTC technology has been made more accessible and economically justified to implement in drive applications. This has allowed for the expansion of DTC into a wider range of applications.</p>
    <p>Ability to respond rapidly to changes in process variables such as pressure, tension, or position using exceptional speed and torque control dynamics has made DTC attractive to wider industrial and process applications.</p>
    <p>DTC can provide protective functions to connected machines, such as overcurrent, overvoltage, and overspeed protection. Tight torque control can optimize the speed controller to damp out torsional vibrations.</p>
    <p>DTC has also been applied to reduce harmonic distortion in the power supply. The DTC drive, hence improving low line quality. Low line quality is a major concern in many industrial applications, and DTC can help to mitigate this issue. Furthermore, the DTC drive can be used with a drive using an IV bus to feed the braking energy back to the grid. This in applications that require frequent deceleration energy cost savings can be achieved.</p>
    <p>powering PM synchronous motors typically supply 5-8 kHz switching to run the motors with best possible dynamics.</p>
    <p>Software has been another key element behind the success of DTC. Improvements and updates include redesigned and optimized control algorithms, which allow for more flexible and efficient motor control. The DTC technology has been made more accessible and economically justified to implement in drive applications.</p>
    <p>Motor models also require regular updating. Control algorithms are periodically analyzed and reloaded to ensure that the drive operates at its best performance. This can include updating some new features or control ideas with an existing or upgraded drive.</p>
    <p>After an improvement has been confirmed, it may be incorporated into the software release as part of normal maintenance. This allows the drive to benefit from the latest performance improvements. It is important to note that the drive’s performance is not limited to a customer problem has enough general appeal, it could be incorporated into the software release.</p>
    <p>A more robust motor identification algorithm has also been developed, which allows the drive to identify the motor parameters more accurately. This allows the drive to operate at its best performance, even when the motor parameters are not perfectly known or prove inaccurate.</p>
    <p>ABB has focused its respected drive engineering heritage on the development of DTC technology. Today, DTC remains a living technology, having built a strong foundation of knowledge and expertise. As a result, DTC has grown into a brand offering larger than “torque control” – incorporating intelligent user interfaces, drive diagnostics, and system-level features.</p>
    <p>Looking ahead, ABB intends to follow the same path with its emerging DTC technology. The company is committed to ensuring that customers can be confident that the benefits of direct torque control technology in which they invest today will continue for the long term.</p>
    <p>For help with any technical terms in this release, please go to: <a href="http://www.abb.com/glossary">www.abb.com/glossary</a></p>
</div>

